  "slug": "untitled-topic-7932",
  "summary": "Generative, MD-based, and FRET-scale methods now resolve conformational, allosteric, and sequence-level structural features at scale.",
  "synthesis": "The dominant thread is the use of computational and high-throughput approaches to link protein sequence, conformational dynamics, and function. Several items apply molecular dynamics or dynamics-aware modeling: temporal hydrogen-bond network analysis of E. coli DHFR and its L28R mutant, supervised MD of RNA-disordered region recognition, and ProteinFlux, a flow-matching model that generates PTM-conditioned all-atom trajectories. Others address allostery, including ubiquitous distal chemical shift perturbations and ZHMolEReP's residue-level allosteric site prediction. Design-oriented work spans environment-conditioned alpha-helical peptide design, generative design of amyloid strains, and FRET selection of millions of random sequences revealing learnable phenotypes beyond soluble versus aggregated. What is new is the scale of sequence exploration and the explicit linking of structural features to predicted dynamics or function, although the items differ in scope and do not share a single method.",
  "synthesis_model": "claude-haiku-5-5",
  "synthesized_on": "2026-05-11"
}
